# What survives when a paper leaves the lab: re-testing {{papers}} robot-learning papers on a €{{rig_eur}} rig

*Ravindu Somawansa, Shisso Robotics. Draft, not submitted.*

## Abstract

Of {{c_paper}} claims taken from {{papers}} robot-learning papers, {{c_paper_repro}} held when we re-tested them on an SO-101 arm driven by a Jetson Orin NX, a rig that costs about €{{rig_eur}}. Most of these papers were shown in simulation or on far more expensive hardware, and it remains unclear how much of what they report survives on the kind of setup a small team can afford. To this end, we keep a register of pre-registered re-tests: for every claim, the pass mark is committed before the run, and the cheapest baseline the method must beat (copy-last, the commanded joints or episode length) is evaluated on the same rows. We find that the failures fall into three classes, which we propose as a working taxonomy: methods that miss the real-time budget of the edge GPU, zero-shot judges beaten by a signal as trivial as episode length, and latent planners that do worse than simply using the commanded joints. The register, every stored row and the code that rebuilds each number are public.

## 1. Introduction

A method that works in its paper is usually assumed to carry over, more or less intact, to a smaller rig. However, the {{papers}} papers in this study were mostly shown on setups far from ours: {{rig_far}} of them in simulation or on rigs above $20k, and {{rig_lt5k}} on a setup under $5k, which is where an SO-101 arm with a Jetson Orin NX 16 GB falls (the appendix gives the rig of every paper). It is therefore far from obvious which of their claims a small team can rely on, and which hold only under the conditions they were shown in.

In this work, we present a register of such re-tests, one row per claim, kept on a single €{{rig_eur}} rig. It grew out of our own write-ups: each re-test was published with its data as it was run, and there are {{l0}} so far, which this paper collects in one table. Consider one entry. An adaptive chunking paper claims that choosing the chunk length from the entropy of sampled chunks follows the phases of the task, long in transport and short at the grasp. On our edge GPU, however, a single such decision takes {{ex_a_ours}} against a budget of {{ex_a_bar}}, so the entry records a fail on this rig; it says nothing about the method on the hardware it was shown on. Next to the verdict, the entry keeps the claim in the paper's words, our pass mark, our measurement and the published rows.

Overall, {{c_paper_repro}} of the {{c_paper}} claims we tested reproduce on this rig and {{c_paper_fail}} fail, for a total compute cost of ${{cost}}. Notably, several of the failures are not near misses against the pass mark but losses to a baseline that requires no learning at all, which leads us to propose three classes of failure (Section 4).

## 2. How an entry is made

Every entry rests on a pass mark committed to version control before its test runs, so that a result cannot move the bar after the fact; Section 5 checks from the commit history how well we kept to this. Before running the learned model itself, we also evaluate the cheapest signal it has to beat on the same rows (copy-last for a predictor, the commanded joints for a learned latent, episode length for a judge). This costs minutes, and in several entries it is this baseline, rather than the pass mark, that the method fails against.

The papers were chosen for the stack this rig runs, namely world models, VLA policies, failure monitors, action chunking and edge latency, so the failure rate describes this register and not the field. To avoid choosing papers after seeing their results, the register file lists every paper we have tested and every paper scheduled next, each entered before its test.

