## 4. Three failure classes

The adaptive chunking entry of Section 1 misses the real-time budget on the edge GPU, although the method may well work on faster hardware; we call this kind of failure class A, the clock. In a second case, a model judged outcomes on this rig without any training on it. Used as a failure detector, a learned reward model reached an AUROC of {{ex_b_ours}}, whereas episode length alone reaches {{ex_b_len}}. Class B covers such judges of success, failure or progress when they lose to a trivial signal. Class C concerns learned latents: a world model ranked the executed chunk first in {{ex_c_ours}} of anchors, against a pass mark of {{ex_c_bar}}, while an action-only baseline reaches {{ex_c_base}}. Class C thus holds latents used to plan, rank or track progress that lose to the joints, to copy-last or to an action-only baseline.

These examples illustrate the classes rather than count them. The classes are a proposal, drawn from the author's reading of the {{fail_rows}} failed claim rows, and a second reader would show whether they hold up. In practice, they suggest an order of checks for anyone with a similar rig: time the method on the target device first, then compare it with episode length or the commanded joints on the same rows, and only then with its pass mark.

## 5. How far the register can be trusted

A verdict is only meaningful if its pass mark was fixed before its result. Notes and commit dates can be edited by hand; commit ancestry, in contrast, cannot be changed without rewriting history that others have already cloned. For each gate, we therefore find the first commit that contains its bar and the first that contains its result, and compare the two (`repro/prereg-check/scripts/prereg_audit.py`, abridged):

```python
def ancestor(a, b):
    return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(RR), "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", a, b]).returncode == 0
...
            elif b[0] == res[0]:
                cls = "same-commit"
            elif ancestor(b[0], res[0]):
                cls = "bar-first"
            elif ancestor(res[0], b[0]):
                cls = "result-first"
```

Table 1 gives the resulting classes for the {{pr_rows}} gates that ran and were logged by 2026-10-31: most are bar-first and {{pr_result_first}} are result-first. A same-commit gate is one whose bar and result entered the repository together, which says nothing about which was written first. A first pass by text search alone found {{v1_same_commit}} same-commit gates, mostly from batch logging; going back to the earlier files that hold their bars moved {{sc_to_bf}} of them to bar-first and {{sc_to_nb}} to no-bar. The audit table in the repository gives the evidence line for every move.

Table 1: Commit-order class of each gate. The audited column is the one the paper uses; the first pass is what text search alone gave on the same gates.

| class | first text pass | audited, 2026-10-31 | audited, today |
|---|---|---|---|
| bar-first | {{v1_bar_first}} | {{pr_bar_first}} | {{pt_bar_first}} |
| same-commit | {{v1_same_commit}} | {{pr_same_commit}} | {{pt_same_commit}} |
| result-first | — | {{pr_result_first}} | {{pt_result_first}} |
| no bar | {{v1_no_bar}} | {{pr_no_bar}} | {{pt_no_bar}} |
| unresolved | {{v1_unresolved}} | — | — |
| gates | {{pr_rows}} | {{pr_rows}} | {{pr_today}} |

The early gates also mixed point estimates with different kinds of interval. We recomputed {{rc_rows}} of them from the stored rows with a single statistic, the episode bootstrap ({{rc_resamples}} resamples, percentile 95 % CI), and for {{rc_flips}} of them the bar falls inside the interval (Table 2). The false-intervention threshold was fitted to meet its rate, so its interval is descriptive only. The register keeps the pre-registered point verdicts and marks these three as fragile.

Table 2: The three gates whose verdict flips somewhere inside its 95 % interval.

| gate | n | estimate | 95 % CI | bar | point verdict |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| colour judge (no learning), accuracy | {{acc_n}} | {{acc_est}} % | {{acc_lo}}–{{acc_hi}} % | ≥ {{acc_bar}} % | {{acc_verdict}} |
| oscillation among failed episodes | {{osc_n}} | {{osc_est}} % | {{osc_lo}}–{{osc_hi}} % | ≥ {{osc_bar}} % | {{osc_verdict}} |
| judge threshold, false interventions | {{tau_n}} | {{tau_est}} % | {{tau_lo}}–{{tau_hi}} % | ≤ {{tau_bar}} % | {{tau_verdict}} |

Finally, all {{l0}} write-ups rebuild from their published rows, and the check for the cold reruns prints:

```text
$ python3 repro/cold-reproduction/l0_check.py
ok  posts: 29 (report 29)
ok  L0 PASS: 29 (report 29)
ok  L1 REPRO: 6 (report 6)
ok  L1 DIFF: 0 (report 0)
ok  L1 classes: {'REPRO': 6, '—': 3, 'SKIP': 8, 'FAIL-ENV': 12}
L0 PASS
```

We ran the cold reruns from a fresh clone with no credentials. {{l1_repro}} gates reran and matched, {{l1_exact}} of them exactly. A further {{l1_env}} stop at the world-model environment, which is not yet published and whose release would unblock most of them; the remaining {{l1_skip}} need a larger GPU, a private checkpoint or the arm, or rely on a step the write-up leaves out. We also checked the citations and paper-side fields of the register against the papers' PDFs, and corrected them. The register names {{ps_ids}} arXiv ids; the ids missing from our mirror of the papers all belong to scheduled tests, and one tested paper has no arXiv id, so it could not be checked against a PDF.

## 6. Limitations

With a single operator, the author, running the rig and labelling the outcomes, observer bias is the main confound; labels were given with the arm at rest but not always blind to it. Where commit order shows it, the pass mark predates the run, so a label could not have moved the bar. For {{pr_unprov}} of the {{pr_rows}} gates, however, the repository cannot show whether bar or result came first, and {{sc_to_bf}} of the bar-first verdicts rest on matching the bar text in an earlier file; a blind second reader would show whether any verdict flips. Beyond labelling, {{rc_flips}} verdicts are fragile under the bootstrap, and the cold reruns cover {{l1_repro}} gates so far, with the unpublished world-model environment blocking most of the rest.

## 7. Related work

Two works evaluate policies on the same arm. One is a standardized real-world benchmark that reports a taxonomy of failures and recoveries for VLA policies [@2606.08881]; the other runs a fleet of SO-101 cells and evaluates policies at scale [@2607.24481]. In simulation, the vla-eval tool runs VLAs across 14 benchmarks [@2603.13966], and a benchmark of demonstration quality studies one method family [@2610.18869]. In contrast to these, we do not rank policies: we test the claims behind methods (judges, world models, latency) against thresholds fixed before the run, on one inexpensive rig. The closest work is RoboSeed [@2610.40026], which scores VLA and world-action models on five dimensions, including reproduction evidence and deployment affordability. Its reproduction-evidence score counts five audit milestones (configuration mapping, task records, task coverage, training records and seeds), and its authors state that the score is not a probability that a result is correct. Instead of such a score, we give one pass or fail per claim on a €{{rig_eur}} rig, together with the rows behind it.

## 8. Conclusion

> **Conclusion.** In this work, we presented a register of {{c_paper}} claims from {{papers}} robot-learning papers, re-tested on a €{{rig_eur}} rig for ${{cost}} of compute, of which {{c_paper_repro}} held. Several of the failures were losses to a baseline that needs no learning, and every row behind the verdicts is published, so that any number in this paper can be rebuilt from the repository (`github.com/ravediamond/paper-register-reproducibility`). Our findings suggest that re-testing a paper on a cheap rig should start from its cheapest baseline on the same rows, with the threshold committed before the run; we plan to extend the register as the scheduled tests are run.

